I think you're on the right track with your solution. The `torch.utils.data.Dataset` API doesn't really support different outputs for train/val/test/predict modes.

The most common approach is to pass an additional argument to your `CustomDataset`, e.g. `mode='train'`. Then you can check the mode inside the `__getitem__()` function and return the appropriate output.

```
class CustomDataset(torch.utils.data.Dataset):

    def __init__(self, ...):
        self.mode = mode

    def __getitem__(self, idx):
        # ...
        if self.mode == 'predict':
            return image
        elif self.mode == 'train':
            return image, target
        else:
            raise ValueError('Unknown mode: {}'.format(self.mode))
```

The problem is that you're just reassigning `p` to be the new tensor, but you aren't actually changing the original tensor.

Instead of:

```
for p in net.parameters():
    if p.device == torch.device('cpu'):
        p = p.to('cuda')
```

Do:

```
for p in net.parameters():
    if p.device == torch.device('cpu'):
        p.data = p.data.to('cuda')
```

In TensorFlow, `tf.Variable` is the equivalent of a tensor that can be modified during training. In PyTorch, you use [`nn.Parameter`](https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/nn.html#torch.nn.parameter.Parameter) for this purpose.

---

The other construct, `tf.variable_scope`, is used to create a context manager that allows you to share variables across multiple layers. This is useful when you want to define a layer that can be reused multiple times, such as a convolutional layer or a recurrent neural network cell. The variable scope ensures that each time the layer is called, it will use the same set of weights instead of creating new ones.

In PyTorch, you don't need to do anything special to ensure that parameters are shared between modules. You just have to make sure that they're defined in the constructor, so that they get registered as module parameters. For example:

```
class MyLayer(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()
        self.weight = nn.Parameter(torch.randn(5))

    def forward(self, x):
        return torch.matmul(x, self.weight)

class MyModel(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()
        self.layer1 = MyLayer()
        self.layer2 = MyLayer()

    def forward(self, x):
        x = self.layer1(x)
        x = self.layer2(x)
        return x
```

In this case, `layer1` and `layer2` will both use the same weight parameter from `MyLayer`. If you want them to use different weights, then you'll need to instantiate two separate instances of `MyLayer`.

Note that there's no need to worry about sharing weights between modules in the same way that you do in TensorFlow. As long as you call the submodules recursively, their parameters will automatically be included in the model's parameter list.

I'm not sure what you're trying to achieve with your custom criterion. If you want to train a model to predict a similarity score between two inputs, then you should use a regression loss like MSE.

If you really need a custom criterion, here's how you could define one:

```
import torch.nn as nn

class CustomLoss(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()

    def forward(self, input, target):
        return abs(input-target)
```

This will work fine, but it won't be differentiable. You'll get an error when calling `backward()` because autograd doesn't know how to differentiate the absolute value function. To fix this, we need to override the `.backward()` method and provide our own implementation of the derivative.

```
import torch.nn as nn

class CustomLoss(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()

    def forward(self, input, target):
        self.diff = input-target
        return abs(self.diff)

    def backward(self, retain_graph=False):
        grad_output = self.diff.sign()
        super().backward(grad_output, retain_graph=retain_graph)
```

Now you can use this loss function just like any other. Note that this implementation is extremely naive and probably won't perform well. In particular, the gradient computation is rather crude: it only works correctly if the output of the network is positive. If the output is negative, the gradient will be incorrect. A more sophisticated implementation would compute the derivative analytically, but this is left as an exercise for the reader.
